        "id": "s13a",
        "script": "Right, the T three code saga. My laptop was running hot, and I started the way I start a lot of tasks now: I gave a very vague description of the problem to Codex to see what it would do. It wrote up something very plausible, a websocket driven main thread stall, several multiplicative paths turning agent streams into continuous decoding and state rewrites. Sounded viable. So I told it to build the thing and test the thing, and it came back very confidently and told me it fixed it. It was a huge PR, more than ten thousand lines, rewriting the network layer and how updates trigger in React. And none of it mattered. None of it mattered at all. Then it fixated on our ultra think composer treatment, this little gradient around the input box that only shows when you use Claude Code with ultra think, which I had completely forgotten we shipped because I was exclusively in Codex at the time. That was the moment I realized how deep a hole I was in, because it was so far from the reality I was experiencing. Its design advice was even worse: make the pulse static, or run it for two and a half seconds. No. This is why everybody thinks Codex is so bad at design. I hated it.",
